**=================================================================================================
** Winkler L-panel, variant 2 of 3: field-split block-AMG (blockamg) on exactly the problem of variant 1.
** The only difference to WinklerL_pardiso.inp are the two linsolver lines below, so both variants give
** the same load-displacement curve -- compare their run times. blockamg preconditions an iterative
** (Krylov) solve with one algebraic multigrid hierarchy per field (displacement, nonlocal damage).
** Units, geometry, boundary conditions and material: model.inp. How to run: README.md.
**=================================================================================================
*include, input=model.inp

*job, name=WinklerL_blockamg, domain=3d
*solver, solver=NISTParallel, name=theSolver
linsolver=blockamg
** every blockamg setting, defaults included -- explained in README.md
linsolverConfigFile=blockamg.json

*fieldOutput
** reaction force (sum) and displacement (mean) of the load line, y component, as histories, exported to CSV
>>perNode, name=RF, nSet=setLoad, field=displacement, result=P, f(x)='np.sum(x[:,1])', saveHistory=True, export=RF_blockamg
>>perNode, name=U, nSet=setLoad, field=displacement, result=U, f(x)='np.mean(x[:,1])', saveHistory=True, export=U_blockamg

** prints the reaction force after every increment
*output, type=monitor, name=monitor
fieldOutput=RF

** EnSight results for ParaView or pyvista: WinklerL_blockamg.case
*output, type=ensight, name=WinklerL_blockamg
>>perNode, fieldOutput=displacement
>>perNode, fieldOutput=nonlocalDamage
>>perElement, fieldOutput=damage
>>perElement, fieldOutput=alphaP
>>configuration, overwrite=yes

** One step over pseudo-time 0..1 (stepLength). The increment adapts between minInc and maxInc (so at
** least 100 increments); an increment that needs more than maxIter Newton iterations is cut back.
*step, solver=theSolver
maxInc=1e-2, minInc=1e-8, maxNumInc=10000, maxIter=25, stepLength=1
** linear predictor: each increment starts from the last converged increment, extrapolated linearly
>>options, name=theSolver, extrapolation=linear
>>dirichlet, name=bottom, nSet=setBottom, field=displacement, 1=0, 2=0, 3=0
>>dirichlet, name=back, nSet=setBack, field=displacement, 3=0
** the load: the load line is raised by 1 mm (u_y = +1) over the step -- the peak load is reached at
** about 0.1 mm, the panel then softens as the crack grows from the re-entrant corner
>>dirichlet, name=load, nSet=setLoad, field=displacement, 2=+1
